WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.430
The Mandalorian has the production values
and watchability of a Star Wars feature film,

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:09.160
as well as lots of subtle references and callbacks
to other Star Wars media.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:14.460
Here are some Easter eggs that even the sharpest-eyed
Star Wars fans might have missed in The Mandalorian.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:19.580
The Mandalorian takes place after the events
of the original Star Wars trilogy and before

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:21.060
the events of the sequel trilogy.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:25.100
There are a lot of different races from a
lot of different planets interacting in the

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:28.360
first episode, and many of them use their
own distinct language.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:33.520
In a bar scene at the top of the first episode,
a fearsome-looking man with an unruly beard

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:38.100
beats up on a blue-skinned alien, the target
our protagonist bounty hunter is after.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.460
"Look at his glands.

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:44.020
I bet we could sell them at the port!" subtitles
tell the audience what the bully says in an

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:44.940
alien language.

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:51.100
The guy tries to pick a fight with Mando when
he arrives, asking if his armor is "real Beskar

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:51.600
steel?"

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:57.020
The language he's speaking is Huttese, the
language of the Hutts, a Star Wars race made

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:00.080
most famous by Jabba the Hutt in Return of
the Jedi.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:05.340
After the Mandalorian captures and carts off
his captured target to collect the price on

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:10.710
his head in episode one, the prisoner makes
small talk with the stoic, silent bounty hunter

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:11.700
back on his ship.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:15.300
He even notes that he was "hoping to be free
for Life Day."

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:21.100
Then, he stops talking when he discovers Mando's
other bounties frozen in carbonite, just like

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:23.020
Han Solo in The Empire Strikes Back.

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:24.620
What's Life Day?

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:29.520
It's the Star Wars universe's equivalent of
Christmas, a time for celebrating and relaxing

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:30.380
with loved ones.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:36.900
It was first introduced in The Star Wars Holiday
Special, an infamous 1978 television variety

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:37.400
show.

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:42.920
It follows Chewbacca's family as they watch
futuristic TV and wait for him to arrive for

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:43.840
Life Day.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:47.400
The special ends with Princess Leia singing
a Life Day carol.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.580
Life Day isn't the only Mandalorian callback
to the Star Wars Holiday Special.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:57.080
We also see the title character sporting a
distinctive weapon that should be familiar

00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:02.340
to longtime fans of a galaxy far, far away:
the pronged rifle he wields at one point during

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:02.900
the show.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.800
It's a cool-looking apparatus with a long
past.

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:11.060
In Boba Fett's first-ever appearance, made
during an animated sequence during the Holiday

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:13.040
Special, he's packing the same weapon.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:17.920
It likely doesn't mean much as far as a connection
to Fett is concerned, but it's a nifty nod

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:19.040
to Star Wars lore.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:23.740
Episode two delivers even more vaguely familiar
entries in the Mandalorian's arsenal.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:28.780
During his battle with a Mud Horn, he fires
a small flamethrower mounted on his arm.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:34.040
That's apparently standard issue bounty hunter
gear, as both Jango Fett and Boba Fett have

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:36.540
been depicted with one in various Star Wars
media.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:41.900
The first episode of The Mandalorian is filled
with enough alien dudes and monsters to satisfy

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:42.760
any enthusiast.

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:47.560
While it would take too long to list every
single alien who appears in the show, it's

00:02:47.561 --> 00:02:51.849
certainly worth pointing out one of the most
surprising appearances of the episode.

00:02:51.849 --> 00:02:55.590
Early on, when the Mandalorian and his bounty
are booking passage back to his ship, the

00:02:55.590 --> 00:03:01.240
Razorcrest, they encounter an alien hidden
behind a pair of goggles and a long snout.

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:06.760
He plays a killer flute, and we never learn
his name, but he should be recognizable to

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:08.620
any detail-oriented Star Wars fan.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:14.520
This fellow was a Kubaz, an alien species
who's only shown up one other time over the

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.720
course of the Star Wars saga.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:21.480
Think back to A New Hope, specifically the
scene shortly after Luke and Obi-Wan meet

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:22.900
Han Solo and Chewbacca.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:28.980
In the alleyways of Mos Eisley, a Kubaz named
Garindan contacts the Imperial Stormtroopers

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:32.871
patrolling the streets to let them know that
he's found the droids they're looking for.

00:03:33.871 --> 00:03:38.140
If it's a Star Wars property, you can be guaranteed
that it's going to be swimming in robots,

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:40.920
or, as they're referred to in the franchise,
"droids."

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:46.420
The Mandalorian is full of bleeping, blooping
droids in the foreground and background, doing

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:49.120
their jobs and staying out of the way of the
humanoid characters.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:50.280
Well, mostly.

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:53.500
"Oh no.

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:55.240
Bounty droid."

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:59.820
In one scene in the first episode, the Mandalorian
walks through a marketplace.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:04.700
One of the objects he walks by is some kind
of red and white R5 droid.

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:10.239
It looks an awful lot like that red and white
R5-D4 model from Star Wars: A New Hope, the

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:12.360
one with an explosively "bad motivator."

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:17.200
Another R5 model appears later, during Chapter
5, in the Mos Eisley cantina.

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:21.220
When the Mandalorian arrives at the fortified
headquarters of "The Client," he's greeted

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:25.620
by a round, red-light equipped droid tasked
with security and gatekeeping.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:30.760
That looks and sounds just like the security
droid Jabba the Hutt used in Return of the

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:32.380
Jedi at the front gate of his palace.

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:39.039
Until now, the Kowakian monkey-lizard has
had just one place in live-action Star Wars

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:43.080
canon: as the snickering pet of unsavory characters
like Jabba the Hutt.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:48.460
They're mischievous, they're obnoxious, they
seem well-suited for villainy...and they're

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:48.960
apparently delicious.

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:53.220
Immediately after the Mandalorian gets his
bounty assignment from "The Client," he walks

00:04:53.229 --> 00:04:56.050
through the marketplace of a desolate-looking
little town.

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:59.780
One alien species stands out: the Kowakian
monkey-lizard.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:04.020
It's rough for him, too, because he's being
spit-roasted while his buddy looks on horrified

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:04.720
from a cage.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:08.460
It's a bit of a horrific sight, especially
knowing that these animals are known to have

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:10.600
a pretty well-developed sense of humor.

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:16.480
While everyone's favorite monkey-lizard, Salacious
B. Crumb, escaped Jabba's insatiable hunger

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:20.800
by having a special talent for being a goofball,
these other creatures weren't so lucky.

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:26.440
In episode two of The Mandalorian, the one
they call Mando is keeping "the asset" safe

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:28.580
throughout a treacherous and violent journey.

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:34.520
This trip includes a run-in with the Jawas,
a race very recognizable to fans of the Star

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:37.500
Wars films for their hooded cloaks and glowing
eyes.

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:42.580
The Mandalorian blasts any that can get in
his path, turning them into nothingness as

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:44.040
they poof away without a trace.

00:05:44.729 --> 00:05:48.600
Later on, Kuiil tells the Mandalorian that
the creatures really don't seem to like him

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:49.320
for "some reason."

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:50.780
Mando replies,

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:54.160
"Well, I did disintegrate a few of them."

00:05:56.060 --> 00:06:00.460
This calls back to a brief exchange in The
Empire Strikes Back between Darth Vader and

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:04.960
Boba Fett, when Vader tells the bounty hunter
to bring back the crew of the Millennium Falcon.

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:08.600
Vader demands that Fett bring his quarry back
with "no disintegrations."

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:14.320
During the evacuation of Cloud City on Bespin
in The Empire Strikes Back, a random guy runs

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:17.840
into the frame, looking for a way out while
desperately clutching something about the

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:20.000
size and shape of an ice cream maker.

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:24.539
Nearly 40 years after the release of Empire,
the third episode of The Mandalorian provides

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:27.780
a full and compelling explanation for the
frozen treat machine.

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:33.180
The Mandalorian returns to the Client to hand
over "The Asset" and to receive his promised

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:36.380
reward: A "camtono" of the precious metal
beskar.

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:40.680
True to his word, the Client hands over the
beskar in what looks like an ice cream maker.

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:43.900
The Mandalorian is a working man before he's
anything else.

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:49.110
And while he's in possession of a tiny, adorable
Yoda-like creature for the first two episodes,

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:52.720
he's also obligated to turn it over to the
Imperials who hired him.

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:58.419
But Mando isn't a monster and he just can't
bear to part with Baby Yoda after having bonded

00:06:58.419 --> 00:07:01.189
with and developed an affection for the little
one.

00:07:01.189 --> 00:07:06.110
So, Mando attempts to retrieve the baby, and
as he looks for him, he spots a piece of metal

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:08.880
pipe propped up against a wall in an alley.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:12.340
Is that just a random piece of space junk
in this post-war galaxy?

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:13.120
Not exactly.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:18.240
It's covered in ridges and designs recognizable
from a very important piece of random junk

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:20.720
in the original 1977 Star Wars.

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:25.300
It looks just like the pipe that Princess
Leia, Han Solo, and Luke Skywalker used to

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:28.280
try to jam the walls of a trash compactor
they got caught in.

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:33.280
While The Mandalorian boasts some star power,
the true breakout character is the one colloquially

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:35.200
referred to as "Baby Yoda."

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:40.260
Just like Mando himself, the little guy has
won our hearts, and his adventures alongside

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:43.080
his bounty hunter babysitter take up much
of the show's plot.

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:47.900
As far as we know, this adorable little creature
hasn't been given a proper name yet.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:52.820
The online community just uses "Baby Yoda"
because it looks and acts like a baby version

00:07:52.820 --> 00:07:53.360
of Yoda.

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:57.940
In the fourth episode of the show, though,
Mando does provide the child with a nickname:

00:07:58.420 --> 00:07:58.920
Womp rat.

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:03.960
It's a playfully delivered pet name, and one
that has significance in the Star Wars universe.

00:08:03.969 --> 00:08:08.939
In Star Wars: A New Hope, Luke Skywalker brags
that back on his planet of Tatooine, he liked

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:12.500
"I used to bullseye womp rats in my T-16 back
home."

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:18.939
While some may argue on behalf of the Enterprise,
the Millennium Falcon is probably the most

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:20.920
famous spaceship in all of pop culture.

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:26.309
As piloted by Han Solo in the original Star
Wars trilogy, its agility and firepower helped

00:08:26.309 --> 00:08:28.420
the Rebels topple the evil Empire.

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:33.450
And yet, it's an old craft, long in the possession
of Lando Calrissian before Han ever had it,

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:38.000
and it certainly endured some damage in numerous
conflicts with Imperial squadrons including

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:39.180
Darth Vader himself.

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:44.700
Speaking of conflicts with the Empire, there's
the AT-ST Scout Walker, which attempted to

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:47.100
lay waste to Endor in Return of the Jedi.

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:50.500
In that movie, Lando refers to the Falcon
as a "hunk o' junk."

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:55.640
When the AT-ST makes its triumphant appearance
in Episode 5 of The Mandalorian, Mando takes

00:08:55.780 --> 00:09:01.320
a page from Lando's book and calls the battered
Scout Walker a "hunk o' junk" as well.

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:04.500
There's one other fun link between The Mandalorian
and Lando Calrissian.

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:09.300
The fourth episode of the Disney+ Star Wars
series was directed by Bryce Dallas Howard,

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:10.520
daughter of Ron Howard.

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:16.020
Ron Howard was director of Solo: A Star Wars
Story, which features young Lando as a major

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:16.520
character.

00:09:17.140 --> 00:09:21.240
Like many planets in the Star Wars universe,
Tatooine has a large and varied population

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:21.740
of droids.

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:26.780
Upon landing at the planet's Mos Eisley spaceport
early in The Mandalorian's fifth episode,

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:31.160
Mando is greeted by a couple of clumsy pit
droids, much like the ones glimpsed in Star

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:33.000
Wars: Episode I The Phantom Menace.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:37.720
In charge of those pit droids is a spaceship
mechanic overseeing the landing bay, portrayed

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:41.180
by comedian Amy Sedaris under a lot of makeup
and a curly wig.

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:46.240
When Mando heads into the spaceport's watering
hole to look for work, he finds more droids.

00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:51.640
This is notable because the establishment
once banned mechanical patrons, as Luke Skywalker

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:53.420
learned when he tried to enter with C-3PO.

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:58.590
Now, years later, droids seem to be fully
welcome there, mingling with the bar's expected

00:09:58.590 --> 00:09:59.980
variety of alien races.

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:04.840
Even the hardworking bartender is a droid,
and one of the same model that Jabba the Hutt

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:08.100
used to oversee his robotic staff back in
Return of the Jedi.

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:12.440
An episode of The Mandalorian involving Mos
Eisley would be remiss if it didn't include

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:16.980
a sequence set in the spaceport's famous cantina,
and "Chapter 5" delivers.

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:22.360
Inside the place that Obi-Wan Kenobi once
called a "wretched hive of scum and villainy,"

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:26.040
Mando chats with a young novice bounty hunter
named Toro Calican.

00:10:26.330 --> 00:10:31.260
The cocky, aloof young buck definitely gives
off a Han Solo vibe, but maybe that's just

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:34.900
because he's sitting alone in a booth in the
cantina with his feet up on the table.

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:39.460
Toro isn't hanging out in the same booth that
Han did, but it's very similar, and just a

00:10:39.460 --> 00:10:43.480
few feet away from the site of one of the
most famous scenes in Star Wars lore.

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:48.320
Mando finds some action on Tatooine, and he
and his new associate Toro Calican pursue

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:51.040
the fearsome assassin Fennec Shand.

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:56.140
We see them zooming off across the planet's
desert surface on a couple of speeder bikes.

00:10:56.540 --> 00:11:00.560
Anakin Skywalker is seen riding a similar
model in Attack of the Clones, just before

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:03.580
he kills the group of Tusken Raiders that
kidnapped his mother.

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:08.280
Mando also encounters some of these sand people,
approaching with caution to ask for permission

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:09.440
to pass through their land.

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:13.400
They get the go-ahead and find Shand, armed
with a monster of a rifle.

00:11:13.880 --> 00:11:14.660
"She's got the high ground.

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:17.000
She'll wait for us to make the first move."

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:18.660
Mando tells Toro.

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:22.960
That is a subtle, winking nod to a similar
thing that young Obi-Wan Kenobi says to Anakin

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:27.630
Skywalker during their big lightsaber showdown
in Revenge of the Sith, when Kenobi claims

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:28.820
he has the "high ground."

00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:33.180
Eventually, the two bounty hunters get the
upper hand and manage to capture Shand.

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:35.800
They decide to keep her alive, because, as
Mando says,

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:37.560
"She's no good to us dead."

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:44.180
That phrasing reminds us of something Mando's
fellow masked bounty hunter Boba Fett says

00:11:44.180 --> 00:11:46.060
to Darth Vader in The Empire Strikes Back.

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:50.940
When Vader decides to put Solo in carbonite,
the bounty hunter reminds the dark lord that

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:53.180
Solo is "no good to me dead."

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:57.760
The Mandalorian has welcomed a number of comedians
to its cast in supporting roles, and Bill

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:00.600
Burr arrives in Chapter 6, "The Prisoner."

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:05.070
The motor-mouthed stand-up is a perfect fit
for the role of Mayfeld, part of the crack

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:11.450
mercenary team hired by Mando's former cohort
Ranzar "Ran" Malk to bust one of his pals

00:12:11.450 --> 00:12:12.280
out of a prison ship.

00:12:12.980 --> 00:12:17.720
On the way to that gig, Mayfeld and fellow
merc Xi'an address the elephant in the room:

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:20.880
What exactly Mando looks like under that helmet.

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:25.500
Mayfeld, imbued with Burr's comic spirit,
jokes that Mando must wear a helmet to hide

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:26.760
the fact that he's a Gungan.

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:32.160
It's a crude reference to arguably one the
most loathed characters in all of Star Wars:

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:36.400
Jar Jar Binks, the goofy Gungan who played
such a major role in The Phantom Menace.

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:47.680
The sixth episode of The Mandalorian is particularly
full of appearances by notable contributors

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:49.260
to past Star Wars media.

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:54.900
One of the members of the prison break supergroup
Ran assembles is a Devaronian named Burg.

00:12:54.910 --> 00:13:00.380
Under all that makeup is Clancy Brown, a character
actor with more than 250 acting credits, not

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:04.180
the least of which is voicing Mr. Krabs on
SpongeBob SquarePants.

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:08.940
He's also done quite a few Star Wars voices,
including Darth Maul's brother Savage Opress

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:13.820
on The Clone Wars, and rebel leader Ryder
Azadi on Star Wars Rebels.

00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:18.440
Another veteran of The Clone Wars animated
series also pops up in episode 6.

00:13:18.450 --> 00:13:23.060
That doomed New Republic trooper on the prison
ship is played by Matt Lanter, the voice of

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:24.560
animated Anakin Skywalker.

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:29.120
Lanter has voiced the man who would be Darth
Vader in more than 100 television episodes,

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:31.800
which means he's played the part more than
any other actor.

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:37.060
The Mandalorian is full of nods to Star Wars'
past, whether they're tributes to the original

00:13:37.060 --> 00:13:39.680
trilogy, the prequel trilogy, or beyond.

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:43.240
In Chapter 6, though, the show also makes
some time to pay tribute to the people who

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:44.710
made The Mandalorian itself.

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:49.340
When the mercenary crew arrives on the prison
ship for the task at hand, they walk past

00:13:49.340 --> 00:13:55.000
a cell that contains a four-armed monkey-like
creature that belongs to the Ardennian race.

00:13:55.280 --> 00:13:58.360
That species is a relatively new addition
to the Star Wars canon.

00:13:58.520 --> 00:14:03.860
The alien first appeared in Solo: A Star Wars
Story in 2018, in the form of agile pilot

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:04.700
Rio Durant.

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:09.600
Rio was voiced by Jon Favreau, the creator
and executive producer of The Mandalorian.

00:14:09.610 --> 00:14:13.900
Those X-Wing fighters who make an explosive
appearance at the end of the episode are named

00:14:13.900 --> 00:14:16.580
Jib Dodger, Sash Ketter, and Trapper Wolf.

00:14:16.580 --> 00:14:21.460
They're portrayed by Rick Famuyiwa, Deborah
Chow, and Dave Filoni, all of them directors

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:23.700
of various episodes of The Mandalorian.

00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:28.920
The opening of The Mandalorian's first season
finale offers a bit of much-needed comic relief

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:31.540
to cut the high-stakes tension set up by the
last episode.

00:14:32.180 --> 00:14:36.620
It also offers a bit of a callback to a joke
from a previous episode about a beloved part

00:14:36.620 --> 00:14:37.820
of Star Wars lore.

00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:41.620
Remember in Chapter 6, when the mercenary
Mayfeld took offense at being compared to

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:42.770
a Stormtrooper?

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:46.350
It’s because stormtroopers are notoriously
terrible shots.

00:14:46.350 --> 00:14:51.250
The two speeder bike-riding Scout Troopers
shown in Chapter 8 of The Mandalorian can't

00:14:51.250 --> 00:14:56.440
hit anything either, even when firing their
blasters in a low-stakes, no-stress situation.

00:14:56.860 --> 00:15:00.640
They're firing at a piece of debris just because
they're bored and they still can't hit a thing.

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:10.810
In the eighth and final episode of The Mandalorian's
first season, viewers finally get an information

00:15:10.810 --> 00:15:15.780
dump of background info on the mysterious
and compelling ex-Rebel fighter Cara Dune.

00:15:16.450 --> 00:15:21.300
One major little nugget, as revealed by Moff
Gideon, is that the former Rebel Shock Trooper

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:25.000
hails from the planet Alderaan, the home planet
of Princess Leia.

00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:29.780
Or at least, it was the home of Princess Leia
until it was destroyed by the Death Star back

00:15:29.780 --> 00:15:31.200
in Star Wars: A New Hope.

00:15:31.630 --> 00:15:35.600
Craving justice for her people would be a
pretty major motivating factor for Cara Dune,

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:38.420
who then decided to become an active member
of the Rebellion.

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:43.160
Because of her Rebel history, Cara also mentions
her fear of getting interrogated with a "mind

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:43.660
flayer."

00:15:43.880 --> 00:15:47.280
That's more of a horror-fantasy term, like
something out of Stranger Things, but in the

00:15:47.860 --> 00:15:51.640
Star Wars universe Cara is most likely referring
to something like Bor Gullet.

00:15:52.060 --> 00:15:56.240
That's the purple-skinned, psychic creature
that painfully interrogated Bodhi Rook in

00:15:56.340 --> 00:15:56.960
Rogue One.

00:15:57.280 --> 00:16:02.980
It's basically an alien lie detector, while
also breaking someone's brain as it works.

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:08.420
In a major moment of The Mandalorian Chapter
8, Moff Gideon cuts a path to freedom out

00:16:08.430 --> 00:16:11.560
of his downed TIE fighter with the use of
a lightsaber.

00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:14.660
But this is not just any lightsaber; it's
a black lightsaber.

00:16:15.060 --> 00:16:20.400
As it isn't cast in a customary neon shade,
this is a very cool-looking Star Wars weapon,

00:16:20.540 --> 00:16:25.600
and also an instant indication that Moff Gideon
is a truly dark force to be feared.

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:31.220
His dark saber is, well, the Darksaber, the
ceremonial weapon traditionally bestowed on

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:32.520
the leader of Mandalore.

00:16:32.980 --> 00:16:38.040
First forged by an ancient Mandalorian Jedi,
it's popped up throughout the Star Wars animated

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:38.540
canon.

00:16:38.760 --> 00:16:43.640
It was last seen on Star Wars Rebels, when
Rebel fighter Sabine Wren passed it along

00:16:43.640 --> 00:16:47.400
to Death Watch leader Bo-Katan to more effectively
fight the Empire.

00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:52.520
The fact that Moff Gideon now has this coveted
weapon is bad news all around, as he's an

00:16:52.520 --> 00:16:53.580
Imperial diehard.

00:16:54.160 --> 00:16:58.580
That means a good guy, or perhaps a lot of
good guys, had to die for Gideon to get his

00:16:58.580 --> 00:16:59.080
hands on it.

00:16:59.580 --> 00:17:04.260
This all poses a lot of questions which can
only be answered by another season of The

00:17:04.260 --> 00:17:04.760
Mandalorian.

00:17:05.260 --> 00:17:07.040
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:17:07.240 --> 00:17:09.940
Plus, even more Looper videos about Star Wars
are coming soon.

00:17:10.340 --> 00:17:13.760
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

